<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Schiller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1759–1805</span> · Also known as Friedrich von Schiller</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Schiller was a German poet, dramatist, historian and philosopher. His first play, The Robbers (1781), was followed by Don Carlos, the Wallenstein trilogy, Mary Stuart and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a>. He taught history at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> from 1789 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1799, where his friendship with Goethe defined Weimar Classicism. His Ode to Joy was set by Beethoven in the Ninth Symphony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller.jpg" alt="Friedrich Schiller" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Graff_-_Friedrich_Schiller.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
